  ["dip-switch-settings","DIP switches: microsteps, current and idle reduction","Most boxed stepper drivers are set with a row of small DIP switches: typically three for the phase current, one for the current at standstill, and four for the microstep resolution — sometimes more for decay, pulse mode ","Stepper Motors"],
  ["step-dir-signals","Step, direction and enable signals","A controller commands a stepper driver with three signals: a pulse for every step, a level for the direction and an enable. In industrial drivers they drive optocouplers made for 5 V (series resistors or a jumper for 12 ","Stepper Motors",["electronics:optocouplers","electronics:logic-families","electronics:schmitt-trigger"]],
  ["closed-loop-steppers","Closed-loop steppers","A closed-loop stepper adds an encoder to a hybrid stepper so the driver knows where the rotor really is. It can then correct or report lost steps, and — in true closed loop — set the current by the load like a servo: no ","Stepper Motors"],
  ["stepper-sizing","Sizing a stepper motor","Sizing a stepper means finding the torque the axis needs at its highest speed — load plus acceleration — and checking it against the motor's pull-out curve at your supply voltage with a 30–50 % margin; then checking the ","Stepper Motors",["physics:moment-of-inertia"]],
  ["servo-motors","Servo Motors and Drives","Closed-loop motion: AC and DC servo motors, RC servos, servo drives and their control modes, command interfaces and fieldbuses, PID control, tuning, inertia matching, following error and holding brakes.",""],
  ["servo-basics","Servo systems","The closed loop, AC and DC servo motors, RC servos, servo drives and their modes, command interfaces.","Servo Motors and Drives"],
  ["servo-principle","What makes a servo","A servo is a motor, a sensor that measures what the motor really did, and a drive that compares the measurement with the command and corrects the difference thousands of times a second. The closed loop makes the motor go","Servo Motors and Drives",["electronics:negative-feedback","hydraulics:servo-loop","pneumatics:servo-pneumatics"]],
  ["ac-servo-motors","AC servo motors","An AC servo motor is a permanent-magnet synchronous motor built for motion: a slim magnet rotor, a three-phase stator and a high-resolution encoder. It gives flat torque to its rated speed and about three times that torq","Servo Motors and Drives"],
  ["dc-servo-motors","DC servo motors","A DC servo motor is a brushed permanent-magnet DC motor built for closed-loop duty — a light armature, magnets that survive current pulses and a tachogenerator or encoder on the back. Torque follows current and speed fol","Servo Motors and Drives",["electronics:h-bridge"]],
  ["rc-servos","RC servos and their pulses","A radio-control (RC) servo is a complete position servo in a small box: a DC motor, a gear train, a potentiometer on the output and a control chip. A pulse of 1 to 2 ms, repeated about every 20 ms, sets the angle; the ch","Servo Motors and Drives",["electronics:potentiometers","electronics:pwm","electronics:microcontrollers"]],
  ["servo-drives","Servo drives and control modes","A servo drive rectifies the mains onto a DC bus, rebuilds three-phase currents for the motor with an inverter, and closes the current, velocity and position loops from the encoder. It runs in position, velocity or torque","Servo Motors and Drives",["electronics:three-phase"]],
  ["command-interfaces","Command interfaces: pulse/dir, analogue and fieldbus","A controller tells a servo drive what to do in one of three ways: a train of pulses with a direction line (one pulse, one step), an analogue ±10 V voltage for speed or torque, or digital messages over a fieldbus or real-","Servo Motors and Drives",["electronics:serial-buses","electronics:optocouplers","electronics:dac","pneumatics:fieldbus-io-link"]],
  ["servo-tuning-topic","Control and tuning","PID control, tuning a servo, inertia matching, following error and faults, holding brakes.","Servo Motors and Drives"],
  ["pid-control","PID control","A PID controller turns an error into a correction from three terms: proportional to the error now, to its integral over the past, and to its rate of change. In a servo drive the terms are spread over three nested loops —","Servo Motors and Drives",["physics:rotational-dynamics","physics:damped-oscillations","electronics:negative-feedback","math:damped-oscillator-ode","math:laplace-transform","hydraulics:servo-loop"]],
  ["servo-tuning","Tuning a servo","Tuning sets the gains and filters of a servo's loops so that the axis is as fast and stiff as its mechanics allow — without ringing, noise or instability. The usual limits are the inertia ratio and the resonance of coupl","Servo Motors and Drives",["physics:driven-oscillations","electronics:bode-plots","electronics:rlc-transient"]],
  ["inertia-matching","Inertia matching","The inertia ratio — the load's inertia as the motor feels it, divided by the rotor's — decides how much torque a move takes and how easily the axis can be tuned. A reduction divides the load's inertia by the square of it","Servo Motors and Drives",["physics:moment-of-inertia","physics:rotational-dynamics"]],
  ["following-error","Following error, overloads and faults","While a servo axis moves, its actual position lags the command: the following error, about speed divided by the position gain. Feed-forward removes most of it. A limit on the error turns it into a jam and crash detector;","Servo Motors and Drives"],
  ["servo-brakes","Holding brakes","A servo motor's holding brake is spring-applied and released by a 24 V coil, so it holds whenever power is lost. It holds a stopped axis — above all a vertical one — while the drive is off; it is not a service brake. It ","Servo Motors and Drives",["hydraulics:hydraulic-safety","pneumatics:holding-force"]],
  ["feedback-sensors","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches","How a controller knows where the motor is: incremental and absolute encoders, resolvers, Hall sensors and tachogenerators — and limit switches, proximity and optical sensors, homing, emergency stops, safe torque off and ",""],
  ["feedback-devices","Feedback devices","Incremental encoders, absolute encoders, resolvers, Hall sensors, tachogenerators.","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches"],
  ["incremental-encoders","Incremental encoders","A disc with evenly spaced lines turns past a sensor and gives two square waves 90° apart (A and B) and a once-a-turn index pulse (Z). Counting every edge gives four counts per line and the direction. The count is relativ","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["electronics:logic-families","electronics:counters","electronics:differential-pair","electronics:schmitt-trigger","electronics:reflections-matching"]],
  ["absolute-encoders","Absolute encoders","An absolute encoder reads a unique code for every angle, so it knows its position the moment it is switched on. Single-turn versions repeat every revolution; multi-turn versions also count revolutions with gears, a batte","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["electronics:serial-buses","electronics:logic-gates"]],
  ["resolvers","Resolvers","A resolver is a rotary transformer: an excited rotor winding induces AC in two stator windings at 90°, whose amplitudes follow the sine and the cosine of the shaft angle. With no electronics or optics inside it survives ","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["physics:transformers","math:inverse-trig","physics:faradays-law"]],
  ["hall-sensors","Hall-effect sensors","A Hall sensor turns a magnetic field into a voltage. Switches and latches with open-drain outputs tell a brushless motor's drive which phases to energise — three of them give six states per electrical cycle — and count g","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["physics:hall-effect","electronics:pull-resistors","electronics:hall-sensors","pneumatics:sensors-pneu"]],
  ["tachogenerators","Tachogenerators","A tachogenerator is a small generator on the shaft whose voltage is proportional to speed — a DC tacho reverses its polarity with the direction. It was the speed sensor of analogue DC drives and servos; today most drives","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["physics:generators"]],
  ["limits-safety","Limit switches and safety","Limit switches, inductive and capacitive proximity sensors, optical sensors, homing, emergency stop and safe torque off, spring-applied brakes.","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches"],
  ["limit-switches","Mechanical limit switches","A limit switch is pushed by a moving part and opens or closes contacts. Wired normally closed, a broken wire looks like a tripped switch, so the machine fails safe; with positive opening, the contact is forced apart even","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["electronics:switches","electronics:relays","pneumatics:ladder-diagrams","pneumatics:safety-circuits"]],
  ["proximity-sensors","Inductive and capacitive proximity sensors","Proximity sensors detect a target without touching it. Inductive sensors see metal through the eddy currents it takes from an oscillator's field; capacitive sensors see almost any material. Design to the assured distance","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["physics:induction","electronics:bjt-switch","electronics:sensors","pneumatics:sensors-pneu","pneumatics:fieldbus-io-link"]],
  ["optical-sensors","Optical sensors and light barriers","Photoelectric sensors detect objects with modulated light: through-beam pairs reach tens of metres, retro-reflective sensors bounce the beam off a reflector, diffuse sensors see light scattered from the object itself, an","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["electronics:photodiodes","electronics:optocouplers","electronics:leds","pneumatics:safety-functions"]],
  ["homing-routines","Homing: finding the reference point","An axis with an incremental encoder or a stepper does not know where it is at power-up. Homing drives it to a reference — a switch, a switch plus the encoder's index pulse, the index alone, or a hard stop — and sets the ","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches"],
  ["emergency-stop","Emergency stop and safe torque off","An emergency stop is a latching red-on-yellow device with positive-opening contacts that stops the machine in stop category 0 (power removed at once) or 1 (a controlled stop, then power removed) — never 2. A safety relay","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["pneumatics:emergency-stop-pneu","pneumatics:safety-functions","electronics:relays"]],
  ["motor-brakes","Spring-applied motor brakes","A spring-applied brake clamps a friction disc whenever its coil is unpowered and is released by an electromagnet pulling an armature across a small air gap — so power loss applies it: fail-safe. Holding brakes keep a sto","Encoders, Sensors and Limit Switches",["physics:solenoid","hydraulics:load-holding"]],
  ["mechanics-transmission","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion","What the motor drives: gearboxes, belts, lead and ball screws, rack and pinion, couplings and bearings; kinds of load; reflected inertia; motion profiles; RMS torque; and a guide to sizing a motor.",""],
  ["transmission","Transmissions","Gearboxes, belts and pulleys, lead and ball screws, rack and pinion and linear axes, couplings, bearings.","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion"],
  ["gearboxes","Gearboxes","A gearbox trades speed for torque: with a ratio i the output turns i times slower and gives i times the torque, less its losses. Spur, helical, planetary, bevel, worm, cycloidal and strain-wave gearboxes differ in ratio ","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:torque","physics:efficiency"]],
  ["belts-pulleys","Belts and pulleys","Belts carry power between shafts some distance apart, quietly and cheaply. V-belts and flat belts grip by friction — the tight side may pull only about e^{μθ} times the slack side before they slip; timing belts mesh with","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:friction","physics:tension-pulleys"]],
  ["lead-ball-screws","Lead screws and ball screws","A screw turns rotation into straight-line motion: one turn moves the nut by the lead, and a small torque gives a large force. Sliding lead screws are cheap, quiet and may hold a load by friction; ball screws are 90 % eff","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:friction","physics:inclined-plane","hydraulics:rod-buckling"]],
  ["rack-pinion-linear","Rack and pinion and linear axes","A pinion rolling on a toothed rack moves a carriage π·m·z per turn over any length, with the same stiffness everywhere — the drive of long gantries. It is one of four ways to build a linear axis: ball screw, timing belt,","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:friction"]],
  ["couplings-alignment","Couplings and alignment","A coupling joins the motor shaft to the load: it carries the torque and forgives a little misalignment — offset, angle and end float. It cannot forgive much: misaligned shafts load the bearings, heat the coupling and sha","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:rotational-dynamics","physics:driven-oscillations"]],
  ["bearings-motors","Bearings and bearing life","A motor spins on two rolling bearings that carry the rotor, the belt or coupling loads and any thrust. Their fatigue life falls with the cube of the load — double the belt pull and a ball bearing lasts an eighth as long ","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:friction"]],
  ["motion-sizing","Motion and sizing","Kinds of load torque, reflected inertia, motion profiles, RMS torque, a motor selection method.","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion"],
  ["load-torque-types","Constant-torque, variable-torque and constant-power loads","Loads come in families by how their torque changes with speed: constant torque (conveyors, hoists), torque rising with the square of speed (fans and centrifugal pumps), constant power (winders, spindles), friction and pu","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:power","physics:friction","hydraulics:pump-head-power"]],
  ["inertia-reflected","Reflected inertia","A load's inertia, seen from the motor through a ratio i, is divided by i²; a mass moving on a screw of lead p looks like m(p/2π)². The ratio of that reflected inertia to the motor's own rotor inertia decides how much tor","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:moment-of-inertia","physics:rotational-dynamics","physics:rotational-kinetic-energy"]],
  ["motion-profiles","Motion profiles: trapezoidal and S-curve","A point-to-point move is planned as a velocity profile: accelerate, cruise, decelerate. The trapezoid is the standard, the triangle what remains of it on short moves, and the S-curve limits the jerk so the machine does n","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["physics:constant-acceleration","math:derivative","physics:damped-oscillations"]],
  ["rms-torque-sizing","RMS torque and duty","A motor in a machine cycle accelerates, cruises, brakes and waits. Two limits apply: the peak torque must stay inside the motor and drive's short-time rating, and the heat — which grows with torque squared — must average","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion",["electronics:rms-values"]],
  ["motor-selection-method","Sizing a motor step by step","A method that works for every motor type: define the task, choose the mechanism and ratio, plan the profile, refer every force and inertia to the motor, compute the torque in each part of the cycle, then check peak torqu","Mechanics: Loads, Gears and Motion"],
  ["real-world","Motors in the Real World","What happens in service: heating and derating, friction, noise, vibration, bearing and winding failures, and maintenance and diagnostics — and special motors: linear, voice-coil, piezo, torque, coreless and vibration mot",""],
  ["phenomena","Heat, friction, noise and failure","Heating and derating, friction, noise, vibration, bearing failures, motor failures, maintenance and diagnostics.","Motors in the Real World"],
  ["motor-heating","Heating, thermal time constants and derating","Every loss in a motor ends up as heat. The winding warms towards a steady temperature with a thermal time constant of minutes to hours; the insulation class says how hot it may get, every 10 °C more roughly halves the in","Motors in the Real World",["physics:newtons-law-of-cooling","math:exponential-growth-decay","electronics:heat-sinks"]],
  ["friction-in-motors","Friction and its costs","Bearings, seals, brushes, grease, the cooling fan and — in magnet motors — cogging all resist the shaft. Friction costs energy, heats bearings, wears brushes and seals, and makes slow, precise motion jerky; knowing how e","Motors in the Real World",["physics:friction"]],
  ["motor-noise","Motor noise","A motor makes noise in four ways: magnetic forces hum at twice the line frequency and whine at the drive's switching frequency, bearings and brushes rumble and hiss, and the fan roars with speed. Knowing which is which —","Motors in the Real World",["physics:sound-intensity","ergonomics:noise-basics","ergonomics:noise-exposure","ergonomics:noise-control","electronics:decibels"]],
  ["motor-vibration","Vibration and its causes","Every motor vibrates a little; how much, and at what frequency, tells you what is wrong. Unbalance shakes at once per revolution, misalignment at twice, looseness at many harmonics, electrical faults at twice line freque","Motors in the Real World",["physics:driven-oscillations","physics:centripetal-force","math:fourier-series","ergonomics:whole-body-vibration"]],
  ["bearing-failures","Bearing failures","Bearings are the part of a motor that fails most often — rarely from old age, mostly from poor lubrication, dirt, overload from belts, misalignment, careless fitting and, on inverter drives, electric sparks through the g","Motors in the Real World"],
  ["motor-failures","Why motors fail","Bearings and stator windings account for most motor failures. Windings die of heat — overload, blocked cooling, too many starts, a lost phase, an unbalanced or wrong supply — and of moisture, dirt and voltage spikes. The","Motors in the Real World"],
  ["maintenance-diagnostics","Maintenance and diagnostics","Keeping motors running means looking after bearings, cooling and connections, and testing what cannot be seen: insulation resistance and polarisation index, winding resistance balance, the current signature of the rotor,","Motors in the Real World",["electronics:multimeter","electronics:wheatstone-bridge"]],
  ["special-topic","Special motors","Linear motors, voice-coil actuators, piezo and ultrasonic motors, torque motors, coreless motors, vibration motors, solenoids.","Motors in the Real World"],
  ["linear-motors","Linear motors","A linear motor is a rotary motor cut open and rolled flat: a coil forcer rides over a magnet track and pushes the load directly, with no screw, belt or gear. It gives high speed, high acceleration, no backlash and no wea","Motors in the Real World"],
  ["voice-coil-actuators","Voice-coil actuators","A voice-coil actuator is a loudspeaker built to position things: a coil in the gap of a permanent magnet feels a force proportional to its current, in either direction, almost the same over the whole stroke. With no comm","Motors in the Real World",["physics:lorentz-force"]],
  ["piezo-motors","Piezo and ultrasonic motors","Piezoelectric ceramics change length by about a tenth of a per cent in a strong electric field — micrometres, with nanometre resolution and enormous force. Stacked, they position directly; driven as stick-slip, walking o","Motors in the Real World",["physics:friction","physics:driven-oscillations","electronics:capacitors"]],
  ["torque-motors","Torque motors and direct drive","A torque motor is a large-diameter, many-pole permanent-magnet motor that drives its load directly, with no gearbox: no backlash, no gear wear, high stiffness and accuracy set by an encoder on the load. The price is a bi","Motors in the Real World"],
  ["coreless-motors","Coreless (ironless) motors","In a coreless motor the rotor is a self-supporting cup of copper wire spinning in the air gap, with no iron. That removes cogging and rotor iron losses and makes the rotor extremely light: fast, smooth and efficient smal","Motors in the Real World"],
  ["vibration-motors","Vibration motors","Some motors are built to shake. An eccentric rotating mass (ERM) motor spins an off-centre weight; a linear resonant actuator (LRA) bounces a magnet on a spring at its resonance; industrial vibrator motors swing adjustab","Motors in the Real World",["physics:centripetal-force","physics:driven-oscillations","ergonomics:hand-arm-vibration"]],
  ["solenoid-actuators","Solenoids","A solenoid is a coil that pulls an iron plunger into itself. Its force grows steeply as the air gap closes — weak at the start of the stroke, strong at the end — so it suits on–off motion: locks, latches, valves and star","Motors in the Real World",["physics:solenoid","electronics:flyback-diode","pneumatics:solenoid-valves","electronics:relays"]],
  ["hydraulic-motors","Hydraulic Motors and Systems","Motors driven by oil under pressure — gear, vane, axial and radial piston and orbital motors — and the whole system around them: power unit, valves, counterbalance and brakes, hydrostatic transmissions, fluids, filtratio",""],
  ["hydraulic-motor-types","Hydraulic motors","How a hydraulic motor works, gear, vane, axial-piston, radial-piston and orbital motors, sizing.","Hydraulic Motors and Systems"],
  ["hydraulic-motor-principle","How a hydraulic motor works","A hydraulic motor is a positive-displacement pump run backwards: every revolution passes a fixed volume of oil, its displacement. Pressure difference times displacement gives the torque, flow divided by displacement give","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:hydraulic-motors","hydraulics:pressure-flow-power","hydraulics:motor-torque-speed","hydraulics:pump-efficiencies","physics:torque"]],
  ["gear-motors-hyd","Gear motors","Two meshing gears in a close-fitting housing: pressure on the teeth leaving the mesh turns them, and the oil is carried round the outside in the tooth spaces. Cheap, compact and robust, gear motors suit fans, conveyors a","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:gear-pumps","hydraulics:positive-displacement"]],
  ["vane-motors-hyd","Vane motors","A slotted rotor carries vanes that slide out against an oval cam ring; pressure on the vanes' exposed faces turns the rotor. Balanced vane motors are quiet and smooth, run from about 50 to 3000 rpm at up to about 210 bar","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:vane-pumps"]],
  ["axial-piston-motors","Axial-piston motors","Pistons in a rotating cylinder block, parallel to the shaft, pushed out by pressure against an inclined swash plate — or in a block tilted to the shaft (bent axis). The most efficient high-speed hydraulic motors: 350–420","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:piston-pumps","hydraulics:variable-displacement","hydraulics:hydrostatic-transmission"]],
  ["radial-piston-motors","Radial-piston motors","Pistons arranged like the spokes of a wheel push on an eccentric crank or on a cam ring with many lobes. They are the low-speed, high-torque motors: large displacements (0.1 to 30 L/rev and more), smooth running below 1 ","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:lsht-motors","hydraulics:piston-pumps"]],
  ["orbital-motors","Orbital (gerotor and geroler) motors","A star-shaped inner gear with N lobes rolls inside a ring with N + 1, orbiting N times per shaft revolution. The many chamber fillings give a large displacement in a small, cheap motor: the everyday low-speed, high-torqu","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:lsht-motors"]],
  ["hydraulic-motor-sizing","Sizing a hydraulic motor and its pump","Start at the load: torque and speed, starting and holding. Choose a working pressure below the relief setting, then the displacement from the torque, the flow from the speed, the pump from the flow and the electric motor","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:displacement-flow"]],
  ["hydraulic-motor-circuits","The hydraulic system","The power unit, directional and flow-control valves, counterbalance valves and brakes, hydrostatic transmissions, fluids and filtration, heat and noise.","Hydraulic Motors and Systems"],
  ["hydraulic-power-unit","The hydraulic power unit","Everything that makes and conditions the flow for a hydraulic motor: the reservoir, the electric motor and pump, the suction strainer, relief and check valves, gauges, filters, cooler, breather and accumulator. Each elem","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:hydraulic-system","hydraulics:reservoirs","hydraulics:accumulators","hydraulics:accumulator-sizing","hydraulics:relief-valve","hydraulics:filtration"]],
  ["motor-control-valves","Valves that control a hydraulic motor","A directional valve starts, stops and reverses the motor, and its centre position decides whether the motor coasts or is locked; flow-control and proportional valves set its speed; crossport reliefs and make-up checks pr","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:directional-valves","hydraulics:flow-control","hydraulics:centre-conditions","hydraulics:pressure-compensation","hydraulics:proportional-valves","hydraulics:servo-valves","hydraulics:load-sensing","hydraulics:braking-circuits"]],
  ["counterbalance-brake-valves","Counterbalance valves and brakes","A load that can drive the motor — a hanging load, a slewing crane, a machine on a slope — must be restrained while it moves and held when it stops. Counterbalance (overcentre) and brake valves meter the oil leaving the m","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:counterbalance-valve","hydraulics:load-holding","hydraulics:pilot-check","hydraulics:braking-circuits","hydraulics:lifts-cranes"]],
  ["hydrostatic-transmissions","Hydrostatic transmissions","A pump and a motor joined by two hoses make a gearbox with no steps: the speed ratio is set by their displacements. In the closed loop a variable, reversible pump drives the motor forwards, backwards and brakes it, with ","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:hydrostatic-transmission","hydraulics:mobile-hydraulics"]],
  ["hydraulic-fluids-filtration","Hydraulic fluids and filtration","The oil transmits power, lubricates, seals the clearances and carries away heat and dirt. Its viscosity must stay in the motor's window over the temperature range, and its cleanliness — counted by the ISO 4406 code — dec","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:hydraulic-oils","hydraulics:iso-4406","hydraulics:filtration","hydraulics:contamination","hydraulics:viscosity-temperature","hydraulics:air-in-oil","hydraulics:fire-resistant-fluids","hydraulics:fluid-selection"]],
  ["hydraulic-heat-noise","Heat and noise in hydraulic systems","Every loss in a hydraulic drive — in pump, motor, valves, relief and pipes — ends as heat in the oil, and the tank sheds only a little of it: above about a kilowatt a cooler is needed. Pumps, valves, cavitation and air m","Hydraulic Motors and Systems",["hydraulics:heat-coolers","hydraulics:cavitation","hydraulics:energy-losses-heat","hydraulics:air-in-oil","hydraulics:npsh","physics:specific-heat","physics:newtons-law-of-cooling"]],
  ["air-motors","Air Motors","Motors driven by compressed air — vane, piston and turbine motors — their curves, control and air supply, and where they win: tools, explosive atmospheres, wet and dirty places.",""],
  ["air-motor-topic","Air motors","How an air motor works, vane, piston and turbine motors, control and air supply, applications.","Air Motors"],
  ["air-motor-principle","How an air motor works","Compressed air pushes on vanes, pistons or turbine blades and turns a shaft. At a steady inlet pressure the torque falls in a straight line from stall to free speed, so the power is a parabola that peaks at half the free","Air Motors",["pneumatics:standard-air","pneumatics:air-motors","pneumatics:cost-of-compressed-air","pneumatics:air-consumption"]],
  ["vane-air-motors","Vane air motors","The commonest air motor: a slotted rotor turning off-centre in a bore, with sliding vanes that form chambers which grow as air pushes in. Light, simple and reversible, from 0.1 to about 20 kW at thousands of rpm, usually","Air Motors",["pneumatics:lubricators","pneumatics:air-motors","pneumatics:frl-units","pneumatics:noise-silencers","hydraulics:vane-pumps"]],
  ["piston-air-motors","Piston air motors","Air motors with pistons: radial motors with four to six cylinders round a crankshaft, and compact axial motors pushing a swash plate. They give a high starting torque from any position, run smoothly at low speed and suit","Air Motors",["physics:torque","pneumatics:air-motors","hydraulics:lsht-motors"]],
  ["turbine-air-motors","Turbine air motors","Air turbines blow jets of expanding air at hundreds of metres per second onto small bladed rotors. Their torque also falls in a straight line with speed and their power peaks at half the runaway speed, so they spin extre","Air Motors",["pneumatics:choked-flow","hydraulics:pelton-wheel","pneumatics:air-tools","pneumatics:isothermal-adiabatic","aerodynamics:propellers"]],
  ["air-motor-control","Controlling an air motor","An air motor is started, stopped and reversed by a directional valve, slowed by throttling its inlet or its exhaust, and torque-limited by a pressure regulator. The valve and hoses must pass its air without a large drop;","Air Motors",["pneumatics:flow-control-pneu","pneumatics:sonic-conductance","pneumatics:frl-units","pneumatics:pressure-regulators","pneumatics:noise-silencers","pneumatics:pressure-dew-point","pneumatics:way-valves"]],
  ["air-motor-applications","Where air motors win","Air motors win where an electric motor would be unsafe, unhappy or overworked: explosive, wet, hot or dirty places, duties that stall and reverse all day, light hand tools and simple variable speed. They lose wherever th","Air Motors",["pneumatics:cost-of-compressed-air","pneumatics:pneumatic-vs-electric","pneumatics:air-tools","pneumatics:air-leaks"]],
  ["selection-applications","Choosing the Right Motor","The families compared side by side, stepper against servo against VFD, and the right motors for pumps and fans, conveyors and hoists, machine tools, vehicles, robots and drones, hazardous areas — and the cost of a motor ",""],
  ["comparisons","Comparing and applying","Motor families compared, positioning versus speed control, pumps and fans, conveyors and hoists, machine tools, vehicles, robots and drones, hazardous areas, life-cycle cost.","Choosing the Right Motor"],
  ["motor-comparison","The motor families compared","Every motor family side by side — power and speed range, torque density and overload, efficiency, control, cost, maintenance and environment — with the questions that decide between them: what supply, what motion, what l","Choosing the Right Motor"],
  ["positioning-vs-speed","Stepper, servo or VFD?","For moving to positions: a stepper counts steps open-loop, cheaply, at low speed; a servo measures and corrects, fast, stiff and with three times its rated torque for peaks; a VFD runs an induction motor at a set speed, ","Choosing the Right Motor"],
  ["motors-pumps-fans","Motors for pumps and fans","Pumps and fans use more motor energy than any other machines. Centrifugal ones need torque that grows with the square of speed and power with its cube, so they start easily and save enormously when slowed by a VFD instea","Choosing the Right Motor",["hydraulics:affinity-laws","hydraulics:pump-curves","hydraulics:system-curve","hydraulics:operating-point","hydraulics:cavitation-npsh","hydraulics:pump-head-power"]],
  ["motors-conveyors-hoists","Motors for conveyors, cranes and hoists","Conveyors and hoists are constant-torque loads that must start fully loaded. Conveyors want gearmotors with soft starts, backstops and sometimes load-sharing VFDs; hoists want a motor that can lift more than the rated lo","Choosing the Right Motor",["hydraulics:lifts-cranes"]],
  ["motors-machine-tools","Spindle and axis motors in machine tools","A machine tool has two kinds of drive. The spindle needs power over a wide speed range — constant torque up to a base speed, constant power above it — from an induction or PM motor on a vector drive, belted, geared or bu","Choosing the Right Motor"],
  ["motors-vehicles","Traction motors: cars, bikes and trains","A traction motor must pull from standstill up hills, then run efficiently over a wide range of speeds: full torque up to a base speed, constant power above it. Electric cars mostly use permanent-magnet synchronous motors","Choosing the Right Motor",["electronics:batteries","physics:rolling-motion"]],
  ["motors-robots-drones","Motors for robots and drones","Robot joints need high torque at low speed with no backlash: servo or frameless brushless motors behind strain-wave, cycloidal or planetary reducers, with a brake and an absolute encoder on every axis. Drones need the op","Choosing the Right Motor",["aerodynamics:hover-power","aerodynamics:multicopters","electronics:batteries"]],
  ["hazardous-areas","Motors in explosive atmospheres","Where flammable gas, vapour or dust can form an explosive atmosphere, a motor must not ignite it — by a spark, a hot surface or an internal explosion. Areas are divided into zones by how often the atmosphere is present; ","Choosing the Right Motor",["pneumatics:air-motors"]],
  ["motor-life-cost","The life-cycle cost of a motor","For a motor that runs thousands of hours a year, the electricity it uses over its life costs tens of times its purchase price. A more efficient motor, or a VFD on a variable-flow fan or pump, usually pays back in a few y","Choosing the Right Motor",["finance:npv","finance:present-value"]]
